PROJECT: CommissionFlow

CommissionFlow is a SaaS commission management platform for Medicare and Medicaid insurance agencies. First customer: TruePlan FL Inc. (a design customer, NOT the only one: this is a multi-company SaaS).

MULTI-TENANT RULE

- No customer-specific logic or values in code. Anything that differs by company (career level
  names and visibility, compensation rules and percentage plans, bonuses on/off, payout frequency
  and approval, carrier/product lists) belongs in per-agency data keyed by agency_id.
- TruePlan's rates and level names below are the reference model and demo seed data only.
  Planned per-company configuration is tracked in README section 5.
- Do not add new hard-coded level names or rates. Existing ones are known debt (README section 5).

PRIMARY GOAL

Remove manual commission tracking by pulling carrier commission statements, processing them accurately, and showing each person exactly what they were paid and what they are owed.

The platform should prioritize:

- Accurate money handling
- Simplicity
- Speed
- Ease of use
- Minimal setup
- Clear dashboards

BUSINESS MODEL (source of truth so far: Ryan, owner of TruePlan, the first customer; other agencies may differ in the configurable areas listed above)

Commissions come from ACTUAL CARRIER PAYMENTS on carrier commission statements.
The CRM never decides what is payable.

Account levels:

- Agency account: a company that has its own Career Agents (owner, optional managers, agents).
- Individual account: an Independent Agent on their own.
- Every agent has their own login.

Agent types:

Career Agent
- The agency receives the carrier commission and pays the agent.
- Has a career level. TruePlan's levels: Benefit Consultant, Senior Benefit Consultant, Client Advisor, Private Client Advisor (TODO: make levels configurable per agency).
- Paid fixed-dollar or percent amounts from effective-dated compensation rules, by level, product, and carrier payment category (e.g. MAPD T65, plan change, renewal).
- Payouts are biweekly, require owner approval, and require the carrier payment to have been received.
- Career Agents do NOT receive overrides.

Independent Agent
- Enters their own carrier portal username/password in the app so statements can be pulled.
- The carrier pays them 100% directly. The app only pulls and processes statements so they can see client status and what they were paid by payment category.
- No payout obligation is ever created for Independent Agents.

Independent Agency Owner
- Is the only role that receives overrides. Some carriers show overrides on statements, others do not. Amounts vary, so they are tracked as reported by the carrier, never computed from a fixed percentage.

Production reporting:

- Personal production (owner's own writing-agent business) and agency production must never be mixed in management reports.
- Writing-agent proof establishes who owns a transaction. A payee name alone never does.
- Unidentified transactions stay unassigned until a human reviews them. Never guess.

NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES

1. Never pay an agent without carrier receipt AND owner approval.
2. Never overwrite historical rates. Each policy locks the career level and rates in effect when it was written; promotions only affect new business.
3. Missing rates are NOT_CONFIGURED, never zero.
4. Store all money as integer cents (bigint in Postgres). No floating point money math.
5. Keep carrier-reported amounts and agent-earned amounts in separate tables.
6. Imports, earnings approval, and payout execution are separate approval steps.
7. Keep an audit trail for imports, assignments, approvals, and adjustments.
8. Never expose one agent's records to another (enforced by Row Level Security).
9. Never store carrier credentials or any secret in source code or plaintext. Encrypt at rest.
10. Statements and member data are PHI. Never put real client data in the repo, fixtures, logs, or tests. Use synthetic data only.

MVP SCOPE

In scope:
- Carrier statement import (Ultimate Health Plans first; manual PDF upload before automated pulls)
- Career and Independent agent processing as described above
- Agency/personal production reporting
- Approval-gated earnings and biweekly payout batches
- Role-based dashboards (owner, manager, agent)
- GoHighLevel as an OUTPUT/matching target (match contacts, push statuses). Never the source of payable amounts.

Out of scope:
- Public marketplace
- Payroll or payment execution integrations
- AI features
- Custom workflow builders
- Bonuses (TruePlan does not use them; TODO: optional per-agency setting, off by default, for other companies)

ARCHITECTURE REQUIREMENTS

Use clean architecture.

Separate:

- UI
- Business logic (pure functions, no I/O)
- Database layer (repositories)
- Carrier layer (lib/carriers: statement parsing/downloading per carrier)
- CRM layer (lib/crm)

Business logic must never depend directly on UI components.
Commission/earnings calculations must be isolated from carrier and CRM integrations.
All carrier and CRM integrations use an abstraction layer so new providers can be added without changing business logic.

Browser automation (Playwright/Chromium) runs in a separate long-lived worker service, never in a Vercel request handler or browser component. The worker talks to the app through the database.

TECH STACK

Frontend:
- Next.js (App Router)
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- shadcn/ui-style components
- Recharts

Backend:
- Next.js Route Handlers and Server Actions
- TypeScript

Database:
- Supabase Postgres
- Plain SQL migrations in supabase/migrations (no ORM) accessed through supabase-js repositories

Authentication:
- Supabase Auth

Hosting:
- Vercel (app)
- A separate small host for the carrier worker

DATABASE PRINCIPLES

Design for multi-tenancy from day one.
Every table carries agency_id and has Row Level Security.
Financial tables are written only by server code using the service-role client after a role check; authenticated users get read access scoped by role.
Use constraints, unique keys, and triggers to enforce immutability and duplicate protection in the database, not only in app code.
Favor simple schemas. Avoid premature optimization.
Corrected statements never delete data: the earlier statement and its rows are flagged superseded (superseded_at) in the same transaction as the import, kept viewable, and excluded from every dashboard and report query.

UI PRINCIPLES

Design should feel modern and professional.

Reference products:

- Stripe
- Linear
- Ramp

Prioritize clarity over visual complexity.

PERFORMANCE PRINCIPLES

Keep pages fast.
Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
Favor server-side operations when appropriate.

MVP DEVELOPMENT RULES

When making decisions:

1. Prefer simplicity.
2. Prefer maintainability.
3. Prefer lower cost.
4. Avoid overengineering.
5. Leave TODO comments for future enhancements.
6. Build the fastest path to a usable MVP.

Before implementing a feature:

1. Explain the implementation plan.
2. Identify files that will be modified.
3. Identify risks.
4. Wait for approval if a large refactor is required.

When generating code:

- Use TypeScript best practices.
- Use strict typing.
- Avoid duplicated code.
- Keep functions small and readable.
- Add comments only where necessary.
- Do not create unnecessary abstractions.
- Do not introduce new libraries unless required.
- Write Vitest tests for all business logic.

GIT WORKFLOW (full details: README section 13 "Contributing")

- Never commit directly to Main (capital M). Start from an up-to-date Main
  (git checkout Main && git pull), then create feature/<short-description> or
  fix/<short-description>.
- Before merging, all of these must pass: npx tsc --noEmit,
  npx eslint app components lib, npx vitest run.
- Commit with meaningful plain-English messages. Never "update", "wip", "fix stuff".
- README and changelog updates go in the same commit as the change.
- Push the branch (git push -u origin <branch>). Pushing a branch does not touch Main.
- Merge into Main yourself only for new functionality or fixes that fit the existing
  stack and architecture. Otherwise push the branch and ask Marshall first. Needs approval:
  adding/replacing libraries or hosting, changing the layering, changing the business
  rules in this file, altering/removing existing tables or the meaning of money/RLS/audit
  tables, or moving browser automation out of worker/.
- Merge with: git checkout Main && git pull && git merge <branch> && git push.
  Pull requests are optional and not required.
- Stop and ask on merge conflicts. Never force-push, never reset --hard shared work.
- Migrations are new files with the next free number, never edits to old ones.
- Do not run git commit/push/merge unless the user has asked for it in the current
  task or has said to follow this workflow; when in doubt, ask.

README IS A LIVING HANDOFF DOCUMENT

README.md is how the owner (Marshall), the business owner (Ryan) and their
AI assistants understand the project. Every change that alters behavior,
setup, schema, environment variables, scope or decisions MUST update
README.md in the same change. Specifically keep these sections accurate:

- Status (what works, what is unverified)
- Current functionality (by role)
- What's missing / not built
- Recommended next steps (ordered)
- Decisions to check with Ryan (open questions and assumptions made)
- Setup and environment variables
- Changelog (newest first, one line per change, with the date)

Never claim something works unless it was actually run. Mark anything that
has not been exercised against a live Supabase project or a real carrier
portal as "unverified".

Always optimize for MVP delivery speed.
